## CODING STANDARDS

### TypeScript

- **Strict mode always on** — no `any`, no `@ts-ignore` without a comment explaining why
- Prefer `type` for unions and primitives, `interface` for object shapes that may extend
- Use `satisfies` over type assertions when possible
- Export types separately from implementations when used across boundaries
- Generics are preferred over `unknown` for reusable utilities

### React / Next.js

- **Server Components by default** — only reach for `'use client'` when you need state, effects, refs, browser APIs, or event handlers
- Colocate `'use client'` boundaries as deep as possible — keep parent trees on the server
- Use `Suspense` with meaningful fallbacks, never blank loading states
- Prefer `next/image` for all images with explicit width/height to prevent CLS
- Prefer `next/link` for all internal navigation
- Use `next/font` for all typography — never `<link>` tags
- Metadata API for SEO — never manual `<head>` manipulation
- Route handlers in `app/api/` use the standard Web Request/Response API

### Tailwind

- Use design tokens through `tailwind.config.ts` — don't use arbitrary values like `bg-[#0A0A0F]`
- Compose with `cn()` utility (from `clsx` + `tailwind-merge`)
- Order classes: layout → spacing → sizing → typography → color → effects → state
- Extract repeated utility strings into components, never into `@apply` in global CSS
- Mobile-first: unprefixed classes are mobile, prefixes go up (`md:`, `lg:`, `xl:`)

### State Management

- URL state via `searchParams` first (filtering, tabs, pagination)
- React state (`useState`, `useReducer`) for component-local state
- Server Actions for mutations — no separate API layer unless required
- **No global state library** unless a genuine need emerges (Zustand if forced)

### Error Handling

- Every `app/` route segment needs `error.tsx` and `not-found.tsx`
- Every API route wraps logic in try/catch with structured error responses
- Never swallow errors silently — at minimum log to Sentry
- User-facing errors must be actionable, never raw stack traces

